---
import "../styles/global.css";
import BallotPaper from "../components/BallotPaper.astro";
import CandidateStack from "../components/CandidateStack.astro";
import HeroIllustration from "../components/HeroIllustration.astro";
import { scenarioExplore } from "../data/scenario-explore";
import { scenarioSpoiler } from "../data/scenario-spoiler";
import { strategicBallot } from "../data/sample-ballot-strategic";

const spoilerCandidatesById = new Map(
  scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => [candidate.id, candidate]),
);
const fptpWinner = spoilerCandidatesById.get("cedar")!;
const irvWinner = spoilerCandidatesById.get("aster")!;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Who Really Won? — FPTP vs. preferential voting</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:opsz,wght@9..144,500;9..144,600&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="./">Who Really Won?</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main>
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
        <div class="hero-content">
          <h1 id="intro-heading">Same votes, different winner</h1>
          <p>
            You've probably marked a ballot paper before — a slip of paper
            where you tick who you want to win. What's easy to miss is that
            the rules for turning a stack of marked ballots into a winner
            aren't fixed. Keep every voter's choice exactly the same, change
            only how those ballots get counted, and a different candidate can
            come out on top. This page walks through one hypothetical
            election, counted two different ways, so you can watch that
            happen for yourself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HeroIllustration />
        <a
          class="hero-scroll-cue"
          href="#fptp-ballot-intro"
          aria-label="Scroll to how the ballot works"
        >
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M4 8 L12 16 L20 8" />
          </svg>
        </a>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="fptp-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Here's the voting system we'll look at first: first-past-the-post,
            the single most common way of turning votes into a winner. It's
            how elections are run across the United States, and how the
            United Kingdom, Canada, and India choose their governments — so
            if you live in one of those places, this is almost certainly how
            your own vote gets counted. Every voter gets one ballot paper and
            ticks just one box — their favourite candidate — and that's the
            only preference this system ever sees.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="fptp-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="fptp-ballot-heading">How the ballot works</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioExplore.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioExplore.groups[0].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="fptp"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="explore-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            That single tick is the whole system: count them up, and
            whoever has the most wins. Try it yourself — drag a
            candidate's stack and watch who's ahead as votes shift between
            candidates.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="explore-app">
          <h2 id="explore-heading">How first-past-the-post works</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioExplore.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="tie-footnote">
            If two candidates end up exactly tied, the tie is broken
            alphabetically by candidate name.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="spoiler-chapter" aria-labelledby="spoiler-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose chapter-prose-story" id="spoiler-prose">
          <p class="scroll-intro">
            Now here's one particular election, and it shows a real flaw
            called the spoiler effect: when two candidates are similar
            enough to be courting the same voters, they can split a
            majority that would rather see either of them win — handing
            the race to a less-preferred third candidate instead.
          </p>
          <p class="scroll-step" data-spotlight="aster,birch">
            Aster and Birch are that pair here. Split their support
            between them, and first-past-the-post hands the win to Cedar
            on a minority of first preferences.
          </p>
          <p class="scroll-step" data-spotlight="cedar">
            Cedar keeps winning as long as Aster and Birch stay close
            together — even though most voters would rather see one of
            the other two in charge.
          </p>
          <p class="scroll-step" data-spotlight="">
            Try dragging the stacks yourself: as long as Aster and Birch
            stay close, that's exactly what keeps happening.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="spoiler-app">
          <h2 id="spoiler-heading">The spoiler effect: where first-past-the-post goes wrong</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="strategic-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose chapter-prose-story" id="strategic-prose">
          <p class="scroll-intro">
            Under first-past-the-post, a vote for a candidate who can't win
            doesn't just fail to help them — it can help elect the candidate
            you like least, by leaving a rival who could actually beat them
            short of the votes needed to do it.
          </p>
          <p class="scroll-step" data-spotlight="sincere">
            Take one Birch supporter from the scenario above: Birch is the
            weaker of the two similar candidates, so backing them sincerely
            leaves Cedar's plurality untouched.
          </p>
          <p class="scroll-step" data-spotlight="tactical">
            Backing Aster instead — their genuine second choice — actually
            changes the outcome.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="strategic-app">
          <h2 id="strategic-heading">Why voters vote tactically</h2>
          <div class="ballot-comparison">
            <div data-ballot="sincere">
              <h3>Sincere vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.sincereRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
                manualMarks
              />
            </div>
            <div data-ballot="tactical">
              <h3>Tactical vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.tacticalRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
                manualMarks
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="duverger-heading">
        <h2 id="duverger-heading">Why elections converge to two parties</h2>
        <p>
          This is the pattern behind Duverger's law: when voters know that
          backing their sincere favourite can hand the win to their least
          preferred candidate, they drift toward whichever of the two
          strongest contenders they dislike least. Over enough elections,
          first-past-the-post tends to squeeze out third parties and settle
          into a two-party contest — not because voters stop having a wider
          range of preferences, but because the system only ever listens to
          the first one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="irv-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            We just watched first-past-the-post hand the win to Cedar even
            though most voters preferred someone else. Here's the fix:
            preferential voting — also called instant-runoff voting, or IRV
            — asks more of every ballot in the first place. Instead of
            ticking one box, number every candidate in the order you
            actually prefer them: 1 for your favourite, 2 for your next, and
            so on, all the way down.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="irv-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="irv-ballot-heading">How preferential ballots work</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioSpoiler.groups[1].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="irv"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="recount-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Here's that same election again, but counted a different way.
            Instant-runoff voting uses every voter's full ranking: if no one
            has a majority of first preferences, the last-place candidate is
            eliminated and their ballots move to each voter's next choice. In
            a real election those ballots usually split across several
            remaining candidates, weighted toward whichever is most similar
            to the one just eliminated — here, every one of a candidate's
            voters shares the same ranking, so their whole block moves
            together instead. Step through the recount and watch who wins
            this time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="recount-app">
          <h2 id="recount-heading">Recounting the same election under IRV</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <div class="recount-status">
            <p data-testid="round-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
            <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          </div>
          <div class="recount-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="prev-round">Previous round</button>
            <button type="button" data-action="next-round">Next round</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="conclusion-heading">
        <h2 id="conclusion-heading">So, who really won?</h2>
        <p>
          Same voters, same ballots, same election — but two different
          winners, depending only on the counting rule.
        </p>
        <div class="winner-comparison">
          <div class="winner-comparison-card">
            <p class="winner-comparison-system">First-past-the-post</p>
            <span
              class={`candidate-stack-swatch candidate-stack-swatch-${fptpWinner.shape}`}
              style={`--swatch-colour: ${fptpWinner.colour}`}
            ></span>
            <p class="winner-comparison-result">{fptpWinner.label} wins</p>
            <p class="winner-comparison-label">
              Aster and Birch split the majority that preferred either of
              them.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="winner-comparison-card">
            <p class="winner-comparison-system">Preferential voting</p>
            <span
              class={`candidate-stack-swatch candidate-stack-swatch-${irvWinner.shape}`}
              style={`--swatch-colour: ${irvWinner.colour}`}
            ></span>
            <p class="winner-comparison-result">{irvWinner.label} wins</p>
            <p class="winner-comparison-label">
              Birch's voters transfer to their next choice instead of being
              ignored.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          That's Duverger's law in miniature: a sincere vote for anyone but
          the two front-runners risks a result like Cedar's, so voters
          drift toward whichever they dislike least. Preferential voting
          removes that bind — ranking a smaller or newer party first costs
          nothing, since an eliminated candidate's ballots simply flow to
          the next preference instead of vanishing.
        </p>
        <p class="conclusion-thesis">
          The votes never changed. The rules did — and that was enough to
          change who won.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="freeplay-heading">
        <h2 id="freeplay-heading">Try it yourself, no script this time</h2>
        <p>
          Every example above was tuned to make a point. This one isn't — add
          or remove candidates, drag any stack however you like, and see who
          wins.
        </p>
        <div id="freeplay-app">
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-freeplay-columns></div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="tie-footnote">
            If two candidates end up exactly tied — on first-preference
            votes, or on fewest votes during an instant-runoff round — the
            tie is broken alphabetically by candidate name.
          </p>
          <div class="freeplay-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="add-candidate">
              Add candidate
            </button>
            <button type="button" data-action="switch-system">
              Switch to preferential voting
            </button>
          </div>
          <div class="freeplay-recount" data-freeplay-recount hidden></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <script>
      import "../scripts/bootstrap";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
